## How SECRET can reach println (threat model)
Three rails. Still **not** equivalent. ELF = rail (1) without depth.

1. **Check / LLVM / C / wasm front-door** (`check_secret_println_sinks`):
   on IDENT that resolves to `println`/`eprintln`, skip at most **one**
   `LPAREN`, then walk until the **first** `RPAREN`. ELF disasm
   (`0x476675`): compare kind to rodata `RPAREN` @ `0x4c43f4`; equal
   → leave the arg loop. **No** `LPAREN` depth increment in this
   function. WT source would not stop there — **not this binary**.
2. **Stmt emit belt** (`c_emit_println`): IDENT scan stops at first
   COMMA/RPAREN/SEMI, then `c_emit_expr_c` (depth-correct) for that
   span, then next comma-arg. Sees **later top-level** args. Misses
   SECRET **in the same arg** after an inner `RPAREN`. No emit-guard.
3. **Free-call emit-guard** (`c_secret_emit_guard` →
   `c_guard_scan_idents`): C-text scan of **full** `args`. This is
   still the 2.9 target rail. Stmt `println` never calls it.

FN classes (wave-2 live):

| ID | Shape | Why FN |
|----|-------|--------|
| F1 | `println((n), tok)` / `println(id(1), tok)` / `println(((n)), tok)` | Walker `RPAREN` closes at the group/call; later SECRET never seen on check/llvm/wasm |
| F2 | Same on `eprintln` | Same check walker; LLVM aliases eprintln→println builtin |
| F3 | `println(("x") + tok)` / `((1)+tok)` / `f(g(), tok)` | Same first-`)` stop; stmt IDENT scan also stops; `c_emit_expr_c` **prints** `tok` |
| F4 | `"x".len() + tok` | Method `RPAREN` stops both walks; emit-c **truncates** to `"x"` (DROP, not refuse) |
| F5 | `oodac emit-bc` any `println(tok)` | **No** front-door → `CALL println` |
| F6 | User `fn leak(x) { println(x); } leak(tok)` | Formal `x` is not the SECRET name — **2.10**, not 2.9 |
| F7 | `println("${tok}")` | String text, no interp — **5.8** |

Z2 is F1–F4. F5 is backend wiring (named HIGH residual). F6/F7 stay
other sprint IDs.

